  "evidence": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "sha256:1a0406bacf590bcfd43c8c944a87b8fbb095df3efea05098295bb5cc62991d10",
      "quote": "This is because the Random Number Generator (RNG) of the estimator is consumed (i.e. mutated) when fit is called, and this mutated RNG will be used in the subsequent calls to fit."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "sha256:1a0406bacf590bcfd43c8c944a87b8fbb095df3efea05098295bb5cc62991d10",
      "quote": "In addition, the rng object is shared across all objects that use it, and as a consequence, these objects become somewhat inter-dependent."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "sha256:1a0406bacf590bcfd43c8c944a87b8fbb095df3efea05098295bb5cc62991d10",
      "quote": "For example, two estimators that share the same RandomState instance will influence each other, as we will see later when we discuss cloning."
    }
  ],
  "decision": "answer",
  "answer": "When the same RandomState instance is passed to multiple estimators, calling fit on one consumes the Random Number Generator (RNG), which is then used in subsequent calls to fit. Since the RNG is shared across all objects, the estimators become inter-dependent, causing one's fit to influence the other's results.",
